ave extra teaching time  have extra opportunities but this is an  immediate response  to give those children that extra boost  but the best support we can do is seeing  children back into the classroom on the  8th of march  which is i think something that all  parents want to see  all children want to see and teachers  want to see as well  so just to pick up that you're saying  you're consulting on a whole range of  things about extending teaching time  does that mean you  are looking at extending the school day  well we recently appointed sir kevin  collins in order to be able to  look at how we do education recovery a  man who's had so many years of  experience  and we're looking at a whole range a  broad range of different options of how  we can support  schools how we can support teachers but  most importantly how we can support  children and that's a whole range  including lengthening the school day  uh well uh he will be looking at how we  can  boost and support children in a whole  range of different  uh manners but it's not just about uh  it's not just about time in school it's  about supporting teachers in terms of  the quality of teaching  and how we can help them okay sounds  like it is on the table though  um i just want to ask about the timing  of this announcement as well this extra  top-up money  um children have experienced right now  330  days of disrupted education since they  were closed in that first lockdown and i  just wonder  if this feeds into you know a wider  theme if you  effectively just doing things too late  at the last minute after they've already  become obvious i mean you announced more  laptops for schools for example  back in april many still don't have them  now you announced the beginning of  january the exams  were going to be cancelled without  announcing a plan of what was going to  replace them i mean are you basically  you know the essay crisis education  secretary you know you only do things  right at the last minute and yo